50:- `setup.sh`, `run_anchors.sh`, `run_command.sh` are bash scripts. The pipeline invokes them as `bash harness/<name>` from one fresh `/tmp/m5-<uuid>` directory on the already-running operator-managed box. They share that directory across reproduce and profile. No dev lifecycle commands are issued. `BOX` and `FETCH` are inherited unchanged by `boxrun.sh`, including injected executables.
52:- `setup.sh` installs/builds the exact published baseline in the stock image; sudo is allowed only for apt. Published source/configuration stays under `baseline-published/`; inputs stay under `data/`. Setup builds and checks only the baseline; the fixed pipeline profiler owns profiling tools and installation. Setup must tolerate one retry in the same directory. Tuning is deferred.
54:- `run_command.sh` contains only the exact measured baseline command and complete environment at the representative scale, including working directory, arguments, CUDA visibility and GPU count. It runs the published binary, saves its output for anchor parsing, and propagates its exit status. `run_anchors.sh` invokes `bash harness/run_command.sh` from the shared working directory without overriding its environment or duplicating the command. Profiling invokes that same file from that same directory. No agent writes profiling scripts or profile outputs.
55:- `run_command.sh` uses paths relative to its working directory, never absolute paths into the run directory, and writes only under `out/` or temporary files. The concurrency probe and evaluation run it from sibling work directories that link the same trees (see Concurrency probe). Harness and repair prompts state this requirement.
60:## A/results/reproduce/ and deterministic anchor scoring
61:- Every reproduce box command fetches remote `out/` into `A/results/reproduce/out/`, and its `<receipt-id>.log` into `A/results/reproduce/`. Logs remain in the agent view. The local fetched `out/` is cleared before each command to prevent stale measurements. The final copy contains the anchor run's outputs. When reproduce passes, the pipeline freezes those outputs on the box: through boxrun, as operation `reproduce-freeze` under the `setup` budget, it runs `test ! -e reproduce-out` and then `cp -a out reproduce-out` in the shared run directory. Like every reproduce command, its fetch replaces `A/results/reproduce/out/`, so that local copy and remote `reproduce-out/` hold the same outputs: the baseline outputs the seal and the test author read. Later stages never write to `reproduce-out/`; remote `out/` after reproduce also collects profiler and probe outputs and further runs of the baseline command, which are not baseline outputs. A failed `reproduce-freeze` (nonzero exit, failed fetch, or timeout) fails reproduce with `error_class: infra`, or `timeout` on a timeout, without repair.
69:## A/results/profile/ and A/profile-packet.json
70:- Profile fetches remote `out/` to `A/results/profile/out/`; logs are `A/results/profile/<receipt-id>.log`. Its inputs are therefore under `A/results/profile/out/profile/`. Remote `out/profile/` is cleared before profiling to prevent stale evidence from passing.
71:- The pipeline uploads trusted `m5/profile_nsys.sh`, `m5/profile_summarise.py` and the collected probe into remote `profiler/`. Through boxrun, it invokes `bash profiler/profile_nsys.sh harness/run_command.sh out/profile <budget_s>`. The profiler reuses `nsys` from PATH or NVIDIA installation paths. Otherwise it tries `nsight-systems-cli` with apt using only source files listed by the probe, without enabling commented repositories or adding sources. If unavailable, it downloads the official Ubuntu 22.04 amd64 CLI package `NsightSystems-linux-cli-public-2025.3.1.90-3582212.deb` from `https://developer.download.nvidia.com/devtools/repos/ubuntu2204/amd64/` into a temporary directory outside `out/` that is removed after installation (the package never reaches `out/` or fetched results), verifies the SHA-256 pinned in the script, installs with `sudo -n dpkg -i` and runs `sudo -n apt-get -f install -y` for dependencies using the image's existing sources without adding repositories. This fixed profiler installation is an exception to the setup-script installation restrictions above. The profiler prepends the discovered Nsight binary directory to PATH. Missing tools, installation failures and collection/export failures emit `M5_PROFILER_INFRA`, write `out/profile/infra.json`, and exit 2; the pipeline classifies these as `infra` without agent calls.
72:- Collection uses `nsys profile --trace=cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample=none` around the shared command without modifying its environment. An inner launcher measures only that command with a monotonic clock; installation, profiler finalization and CSV export are excluded. The command's output is preserved in `baseline.log` as well as its own saved logs. Export uses `nsys stats --report cuda_gpu_kern_sum,cuda_gpu_mem_time_sum,cuda_api_sum --format csv`. Raw report, CSV files, tool version, export log, `raw_wall_s.json` and `gpu_util_raw.jsonl` remain in `out/profile/`.
73:- `python3 m5/profile_summarise.py <out_dir>` deterministically writes the three existing profile files. CSV total time/duration units ns, us/µs, ms and s are converted to seconds. Known Nsight skips for absent kernel/memory/API activity count as zero; missing or malformed reports fail parsing. A report is a known skip when its CSV file carries Nsight's `SKIPPED` line and no header row, or when its CSV file is absent, empty or whitespace-only and `stats.log` in the same directory states that the report does not contain its own activity: `does not contain CUDA kernel data` for `stats_cuda_gpu_kern_sum.csv`, `does not contain CUDA memory data` for `stats_cuda_gpu_mem_time_sum.csv`, and `does not contain CUDA API data` for `stats_cuda_api_sum.csv` (U48). Nsight Systems 2025 writes an empty file for a skipped report where earlier versions wrote none. An absent, empty or whitespace-only file without that statement for its own activity, and any other missing or malformed report, still fails parsing. Run evidence (blind-01 run9, 2026-11-01): the baseline ran to completion under the profiler with no CUDA activity and no capture problem; Nsight 2025 logged the three skips and wrote three empty summary files, and the summariser failed on a missing CSV header, so profile stopped with `parse` instead of reaching its numeric gate. Five rows are emitted: `kernels` (compute), `memcpy H2D` (transfer), `memcpy D2H` (transfer), `CUDA API host time` (other), and `other host time` (other). GPU seconds retain the raw kernel/transfer sums; host GPU seconds are null. Aggregate CSV reports do not identify critical-path intervals: if the four reported sums exceed measured wall, their wall allocations are scaled proportionally to fit. Other host time is the nonnegative wall remainder. These allocations are an aggregate approximation, not measured exclusive phase durations. No stage timestamps are invented; aggregate rows omit `start_s`, so stage utilisation remains null. No bound uses stage utilisation; same-GPU headroom comes from the concurrency probe.
74:- `stages.json` is an array of `{stage, wall_s, gpu_s}`, optionally including `kind`: `compute`, `transfer`, or `other`, and `start_s`: finite nonnegative elapsed seconds sharing the telemetry clock. Fixed profiler supplies `kind`; legacy rows without it remain accepted. `stage` is a non-empty string; `wall_s` is finite nonnegative seconds; `gpu_s` is finite nonnegative seconds or null and must be present. Fixed profiler wall allocations sum to measured wall without exceeding it; overlapping aggregate durations use the approximation above. Extra input fields are not copied to the packet. Empty arrays parse but fail the numeric gate.
77:- The deterministic pipeline writes `A/profile-packet.json` with exact keys:
83:  - `concurrency`: the validated `concurrency.json` object (see Concurrency probe), or null when the probe did not run or its output is missing or invalid.
84:- Profile facts are `{profile_complete, timed_fraction, gpu_telemetry}` plus common `tokens_known` and `gpu_busy_known`, plus optional `headroom`: the packet's `concurrency.factor` (finite positive ratio) or null. Every executed profile stage emits `headroom`; it is optional in the v2 schema so older records stay valid, and it is null on SKIPPED/stub records. `profile_complete` is true when the command exits 0 and all three inputs validate, even when the numeric gate fails. The deterministic gate requires at least three stages, no process wrappers, `timed_fraction >= 0.9`, and `gpu_telemetry=true`. A stage is a process wrapper if its name contains a case-insensitive token `process`, `wrapper`, `telemetry`, `total`, or `overall` delimited by whitespace, underscores, hyphens, or string boundaries, or if its `wall_s` is within 2% of `total_wall_s` (inclusive). Any wrapper fails the gate, even alongside three internal stages. Numeric gate failures preserve the packet and use `error_class: numeric`; missing or invalid files use `error_class: parse`. A failed gate or parse stops downstream work without a profile repair.
86:## Concurrency probe: calibration of same-GPU headroom
87:- Purpose: measure, before any prediction or method exists, how much more throughput one GPU gives when the unchanged published command runs several times at once. This measured factor is the only source of same-GPU headroom for bounds. No model call is involved.
88:- When the profile command exits 0 and the profile gate passes, the pipeline uploads trusted `m5/concurrency_probe.py` to remote `profiler/` and, through boxrun as operation `concurrency` with the resolved `profile` budget, runs `python3 -I profiler/concurrency_probe.py harness/run_command.sh out/concurrency <budget_s>` from the shared run directory. Remote `out/concurrency/` is cleared first. Fetched files land in `A/results/profile/out/concurrency/`; the log is `A/results/profile/<receipt-id>.log`. There is no agent repair and no execution retry. Probe receipts count toward profile v1 `usd` and `gpu_s` like other box receipts.
89:- Each invocation runs `bash harness/run_command.sh` with its working directory set to a fresh `concurrency-work/<label>/` under the shared run directory. That directory holds a symlink to every top-level entry of the shared run directory except `out`, `profiler`, `concurrency-work` and `evaluation-work`, plus an empty `out/` directory. Labels are `single-1`, `single-2`, `single-3`, `n2-1`, `n2-2`, `n4-1` to `n4-4`. Each invocation's stdout and stderr go to `out/concurrency/logs/<label>.log` of the shared run directory. The environment is inherited unchanged apart from the MPS variables below. Nsight is not used.
90:- MPS: when `nvidia-cuda-mps-control` is on PATH, the probe starts a user-level daemon (`nvidia-cuda-mps-control -d`) with `CUDA_MPS_PIPE_DIRECTORY` and `CUDA_MPS_LOG_DIRECTORY` set to fresh directories under `concurrency-work/`, passes both variables to every invocation, and stops the daemon (`echo quit | nvidia-cuda-mps-control` with the same variables) before exiting, including after failures and on SIGTERM. If the daemon fails to start or any invocation fails under MPS, the probe stops the daemon, records `mps_failed: true`, and repeats the complete probe once without MPS in fresh work directories. Without the binary there is no MPS and `mps_failed` is false.
91:- MPS use is verified, not assumed. The daemon's `control.log` in its `CUDA_MPS_LOG_DIRECTORY` records each client connection as a line containing `NEW CLIENT <pid> from user <uid>`. After each single run and each batch under MPS, the probe counts these lines (case-sensitive substring `NEW CLIENT `, followed by an integer pid and ` from user `). The count must have grown by at least the number of copies just run (1 for a single run, n for a batch of n). A missing or unreadable log, or a smaller increase, means the copies did not run under MPS: the probe treats this like an invocation failure under MPS (stop the daemon, `mps_failed: true`, repeat the complete probe once without MPS). `mps: true` is therefore recorded only when every counted measurement was verified to run under MPS. No log content enters `concurrency.json`.
93:- `out/concurrency/concurrency.json` is written atomically with exactly `{mps, mps_failed, single_wall_s, batches, factor}`:
99:- The probe never fails the profile stage and never changes the profile gate. A probe timeout, nonzero exit, or missing or invalid file gives packet `concurrency: null` and `headroom: null`. The pipeline validates the file against the exact shape above before copying it into the packet.
101:## C/receipts/ and stage failure signatures
102:- Every box command (uploads, probe, setup attempts, anchor execution, profiling, implementation) runs through `m5/boxrun.sh`; every receipt is `C/receipts/NNNN-<operation>.json`. Operations are `upload`, `probe`, `setup`, `anchors`, `profile`, `concurrency`, `implement-baseline`, `adapter-smoke`, `implement-state`, `implement-upload`, `implement-patch`, `setup-method`, `run-method`, `run-tests`; numbering continues across existing receipts. Receipt/log IDs contain no benchmark labels.
104:- Failed reproduce/profile signatures have fixed `error_class`: `infra` for unavailable box execution/receipts, failed upload, or failed fetch after a successful command, or a fixed profiler infrastructure marker; `install` for failed setup after the bounded repairs; `anchor` for an anchor-run failure or a failed anchor; `parse` for missing/invalid output files, unavailable harness files, or a nonzero profile command exit; `numeric` for a parsed profile that fails the stage-count, process-wrapper, coverage, or GPU telemetry gate. Exit codes are 4 for anchor failures, 3 for timeouts and 2 for the other failures. `infra` is added to the shared v1 error-class enum so the v2 envelope remains v1-valid. Box/fetch stdout and stderr are never copied into operator signatures.
105:- Any box receipt with rc=124 or timed_out=true records a timeout, even when fetch fails. A final timeout fails its stage with `error_class: timeout`, exit 3. Authored setup and anchor scripts consume their existing repairs (at most four for setup, two for anchors), and method/test scripts their one bounded repair; method/test timeout repairs expose only the fixed timeout count plus budget/elapsed/overrun seconds, never test content. Transport, patch, probe, integrity and immutable evaluator commands permit one identical retry under the same budget (under keys `setup`, `anchors` and `profile`, the budget resolved at the retry's start, U47), without editing scientific payloads or trusted scorers. A second timeout stops that operation and stage. The fixed profiler permits no repair or execution retry; its first timeout stops profiling with `error_class: timeout`.
106:- Reproduce, profile, implement and evaluate facts optionally add exactly typed timeout fields `{timed_out: boolean, budget_s: nonnegative integer, overrun: boolean}` when a box timeout occurs. `timed_out=true` records any attempt timing out, `budget_s` is the latest timed-out command's budget, and `overrun` is true when any timed-out receipt overruns. These fields survive a successful bounded repair; stage status then reflects the repaired result. Older records and SKIPPED/stub records retain their existing fact keys. No command output or new free-text fields enter signatures.
107:- Numeric ratio facts accept finite JSON numbers, including integer spellings such as `0` and `1`; booleans remain invalid. This permits fully timed profiles to emit the capped fraction `1`.
110:- Stages aggregate reported input/output counts into v1 `tokens_in`/`tokens_out`; `facts.tokens_known` is true only when every runner attempt in that stage reports usage. With no attempts, existing unknown/null facts remain unchanged. Unknown counts retain numeric zero placeholders required by v1. `gpu_busy_known` reflects box receipt telemetry for reproduce/profile (see Compute metadata); harness profile evidence is reported separately. Downstream scientific stages are skipped after either failed gate; takeaways still runs.
112:## A/bounds.json (computed before the seal agent)
113:- `python3 m5/bounds.py <profile-packet.json> <out.json>` writes an atomic JSON object keyed by the five menu methods. Each value is `{p, k, bound, feasible, k_derivation, stages}`. `stages` contains actionable rows `{stage, kind, wall_fraction, mean_gpu_util, methods}`; `methods` lists every menu method that acts on that stage: whole-workload methods act on every stage, the others on stages of their kind. Exit 0 means success; exit 1 means invalid/missing input or unavailable output, with content-free diagnostics. Legacy packets without `gpu_count` default to 1. GPU counts must be nonnegative integers.
125:- `factor` is the packet's `concurrency.factor`: the same-GPU concurrent throughput measured during calibration (see Concurrency probe). When the packet has no `concurrency` key, `concurrency` is null, or its factor is null or below 1, whole-workload methods use `k=1` (bound 1, infeasible): missing measurements never manufacture headroom. A `concurrency` value that is neither null nor an object, or a factor that is neither null nor a finite number, fails parsing.
127:- `k_derivation`: whole-workload methods record `{rule:"min(fixed_cap, concurrency factor)", fixed_cap, factor, mps}` (`factor` and `mps` null when unavailable); pipelining records `{rule:"fixed_cap", fixed_cap:2}`; multi-GPU records `{rule:"max(1, gpu_count)", gpu_count, amdahl, cost_basis:"equal_gpu_seconds"}`. Stage `mean_gpu_util` is carried for information only and never sets a factor.
128:- These are ceilings for sealed intervals, not measured method improvements. The concurrency factor is the measured throughput of plain concurrent runs; method code may exceed it in evaluation, but sealed intervals may not. `feasible` is true exactly when `bound >= 1.05`.
129:- The seal and propose prompts describe the bounds this way: whole-workload bounds are measured concurrent throughput on one GPU; pipelining is Amdahl over transfer time; multi-GPU bounds are per GPU-second at equal cost.
130:- The pipeline regenerates `A/bounds.json` before sealing, reads it before the agent call, and supplies its path and contents in the seal prompt. Missing or invalid profile evidence fails sealing; stale bounds cannot substitute for it.
132:## A/PREDICTIONS.json (written by the seal agent; checked, then sealed)
138:- The seal agent reads `A/intake/`, `A/profile-packet.json`, `A/bounds.json` and the baseline's measured outputs under `A/results/reproduce/` (the fetched `out/`, logs and `anchors_scored.json`), and nothing else. A margin derived from baseline variability names, in its `source`, the measured file and the statistic used (for example the standard deviation of a per-seed outcome across the baseline's seeds). When neither a scientific requirement nor measured baseline variability supports a positive margin, the seal agent never invents one, and the seal fails `stochastic_equivalence_invalid` as before. The seal prompt states all three.
142:- Seal failures use `error_class: numeric` (exit 2), including invalid protocol, invalid intervals, missing/duplicate method coverage, or unavailable bounds. Facts retain exactly `seal_present`, `seal_verified`, `prediction_count` plus common facts; `prediction_count` counts submitted prediction rows, excluding infeasible entries. An empty feasible menu may be sealed as a negative result: a verified seal with zero predictions (every menu method listed in `infeasible_methods`) PASSes the seal stage with `prediction_count=0` and `reason_codes: []`. Propose then decides whether the absence is a negative finding or missing measurements.
143:- The seal prompt embeds stochastic and deterministic JSON skeletons generated by `m5/seal_protocol.js`, using the same constants as its deterministic validation. Both specify `equivalence_metrics=[{name,source,tolerance:{abs:number}}]` with non-empty **string** names and sources, never citation objects. The stochastic example uses `paired_seed_tests={kind:"paired_median",seeds:[0,1,2,3,4,5]}` and a positive absolute margin; the deterministic example uses `{kind:"deterministic",justification:"scientific reason"}` and zero absolute tolerance. Both include `evaluation_inputs={ids:["held-out-1"]}`, matching `sample_sizes={inputs:1,seeds:6}` (stochastic) or `{inputs:1,seeds:1}` (deterministic), `confidence_rule={performance:{kind:"paired_median",confidence:0.95},equivalence:{kind:"paired_median",confidence:0.95}}`, and `baseline_invocation_s={seconds:<placeholder number>,source:"<placeholder>"}`. Example metric names, sources, margins, identities and justifications must be replaced with supported scientific choices. Six seeds give finite two-sided 95% bounds for one metric; more metrics or higher confidence can require more seeds. Deterministic evaluation uses one null seed.
144:- One bounded repair is allowed for a readable but invalid protocol or a missing/malformed predictions file, before any immutable seal files are written. Initial `seal` and optional `seal-repair` calls use `ls-writer`, requested model `sonnet`, `repairable:false`, `fresh:true`; runner-level repair is disabled. The repair prompt supplies only script-owned failing reason codes and field paths as validation feedback, without values, evidence, bounds, measurements, exception text or model commentary. The agent may read and rewrite only `A/PREDICTIONS.json`, preserving scientific intent without inventing evidence or loosening tolerances. The complete deterministic checks run again exactly once; failure stops, success proceeds to the existing seal writer. Bounds generation, agent execution, seal storage/verification failures and verified negative results do not trigger repair. Repair never allocates a seal number or modifies prior receipts/frozen predictions. No field paths or feedback enter operator signatures. For `protocol_cost` the field paths are `warmup`, `repetitions`, `paired_seed_tests.seeds`, `evaluation_inputs.ids`, `compute_budgets` and `baseline_invocation_s`, and the repair prompt also restates the resolved `evaluate` budget in seconds and the projection rule from the seal prompt; it supplies no other value.
149:  | `bounds_generation_failed` | Regenerate and read bounds from the profile packet. |
157:  | `bounds_coverage_invalid` | Feasible prediction coverage or infeasible exclusions/reasons are invalid. |
158:  | `interval_bounds_invalid` | An interval exceeds its bound or its allowed width. |
165:## Propose, independent votes, and the one-shot gate
166:- Before reading profile/bounds or calling any agent, propose selects the highest-numbered `C/seals/NNNN.json` and hashes the exact bytes of both `A/PREDICTIONS.json` and the receipt's frozen `path`. Both must match `sha256`. A missing, unreadable, or mismatched seal fails with `error_class: other` (exit 2), with no model calls.
167:- The deterministic step reads `A/profile-packet.json`, `A/bounds.json`, and the verified predictions. Feasible predicted methods have `bounds[method].feasible=true` and `bound >= 1.05`. Their ranking score is `min(low/2 + high/2, bound)`, descending; ties follow the fixed menu order. Unknown/duplicate methods or malformed intervals fail parsing.
168:- Resource guards use `C/box.json.gpu_count` when supplied (a nonnegative integer), otherwise the count in allocation `type` such as `gpu_1x_a10`. Probe capacity is `A/results/probe/probe.json.nvidia.gpus.length`. Available GPUs are the smaller count; either inventory unavailable means no method passes. Ordinary methods require one GPU; multi-GPU requires `max(2, bounds[method].k)` GPUs, matching the factor used for its bound. Methods failing these guards are excluded from selection.
169:- `A/propose-packet.json` contains only `{ranked_methods, stage_to_method, bounds, sealed_equivalence_rule}`. Ranked rows are `{method,bounds}` in deterministic ranking order, restricted to feasible resource-safe methods. `stage_to_method` contains every stage as `{stage,wall_fraction,mean_gpu_util,methods}`; other stages map to an empty method list. `bounds` contains all five bounds and their `k_derivation` and actionable stages. `sealed_equivalence_rule` contains only `outputs_stochastic`, `equivalence_metrics`, `paired_seed_tests`, `sample_sizes`, and the equivalence portion of `confidence_rule`. No raw profile, predicted outcome curves, intake, resource inventory, performance protocol, or other content enters the packet. Prompts state that ranked methods already passed deterministic GPU resource guards. Proposer and all five skeptics receive the identical packet; skeptics additionally receive the validated proposal. When no feasible resource-safe method remains (including a verified seal with zero predictions), propose makes no model calls, writes `A/propose-packet.json` and `A/GATE.json` as usual, and keeps `method="none"`, `expected_speedup=null` and `gate_pass=false`. The absence is a negative finding exactly when it rests on measurements: the profile packet's `concurrency.factor` is a finite number; its MPS state is verified, meaning `concurrency.mps` is true, or `concurrency.mps` and `concurrency.mps_failed` are both false (MPS absent from the box); and both GPU inventories used by the resource guards (box and probe) are available with at least one GPU each. Then the stage PASSes with `outcome="no_feasible_method"`; implement and evaluate are SKIPPED with null facts, takeaways runs, and the run's stop point is propose. Otherwise (concurrency missing, null or not finite; `concurrency.mps_failed` true, meaning MPS exists on the box but the measurement could not use it; or either GPU inventory unavailable or reporting zero GPUs) the absence reflects missing measurements: the stage FAILs with `numeric` (exit 2) as before and `outcome` stays null. The finding is bounded: no method on the fixed menu has measured headroom of at least 1.05 on this hardware (concurrency factor of plain concurrent copies, GPU count, measured transfer share). It does not show that no method could speed up the workload.
170:- One `propose` call (`ls-writer`, requested model `sonnet`) reads only the embedded packet and writes `A/proposal.json`, with exactly `{method, rationale, plan_steps, files_expected, risks}`. `method` must be in the ranked list; `rationale` is a non-empty string; the remaining fields are arrays of non-empty strings. `plan_steps` and `files_expected` must be non-empty; `risks` may be empty. The call cannot implement code or access the box. Invalid/missing proposals fail with `error_class: parse` (exit 2).
172:- Ballots have exactly `{vote, reason_codes}`. `vote` is `approve`, `reject`, or `abstain`; `reason_codes` is an array (possibly empty) containing only `exceeds_bound`, `not_parallelisable`, `changes_method`, `equivalence_risk`, `resource_mismatch`, `weak_profile_evidence`, or `other`. The pipeline writes `A/votes/1.json` through `5.json`; missing/failed/invalid responses are written as JSON `null` (null ballots). No vote repair or replacement is allowed.
173:- Ballot meanings. `reject` names a defect that would make the proposed method fail as planned: it exceeds its bound, cannot be parallelised as planned, changes the sealed method, risks breaking the sealed equivalence rule, or mismatches the resources (codes `exceeds_bound`, `not_parallelisable`, `changes_method`, `equivalence_risk`, `resource_mismatch`, or `other`). `abstain` means the packet is insufficient to judge (typically `weak_profile_evidence`); it is not a judgement against the method. `approve` means no defect is found and the evidence is adequate. The skeptic prompt states these three meanings and that missing or weak evidence warrants abstain, not reject. The gate counts only the `vote` field; reason codes never reclassify a ballot. Skeptic review records evidence about the proposal; a vote count is not scientific ground truth, and execution plus independent evaluation decide the claim.
174:- These calls use wf option `repairable:false`: no repair turn, schema/no-JSON failures cannot trigger fallback, panels are bypassed, and shadow mode becomes fallback. Transport failures can still use the configured family chain. This preserves existing default behavior for other calls. `fresh:true` bypasses the resume cache for proposer and skeptics; every ballot starts a new runner session.
175:- `A/GATE.json` records the propose facts. Its presence prevents another proposal/vote round in the same run (fails with `other`). Gate requires at most one ballot that is a rejection or null (abstentions never count against the gate, and no minimum number of approvals applies), the selected sealed interval's `high >= minimum_worthwhile_gain`, `high <= bound`, and passing resource guards. Gate failures use `numeric` (exit 2); malformed inputs use `parse`. Downstream stages skip after failure; takeaways still runs.
177:- Propose facts add optional `votes_reject` and `votes_abstain` (nonnegative integers) and `outcome` (`"approved"`, `"rejected"`, `"conditions_unmet"`, `"no_feasible_method"`, or null). They are optional in the v2 schema so older records stay valid; every executed propose stage emits all three, initialised to 0, 0 and null, and SKIPPED/stub records hold null. Null ballots number `5 - votes_approve - votes_reject - votes_abstain`. Once ballots are counted: `outcome="approved"` exactly when the gate passes; `"rejected"` when two or more ballots are rejections or null; `"conditions_unmet"` when the ballot condition holds but an interval or resource condition fails. `"no_feasible_method"` is set only by the measured negative finding above, with all ballot counts 0. `outcome` stays null when the stage stops before ballots are counted for any other reason (seal, packet, proposal or parse failures, no feasible method without measurements, or an existing `A/GATE.json`). `OPERATOR_TAKEAWAY.md` shows `outcome` and the four ballot counts (approve, reject, abstain, null) in the propose row. Its null-ballot count is `5 - votes_approve - votes_reject - votes_abstain` only when ballots were counted (outcome `approved`, `rejected` or `conditions_unmet`); otherwise it shows null, because no ballot was requested. When propose has `outcome="no_feasible_method"`, it also adds the fixed line `Finding: negative. No menu method has measured headroom of at least 1.05 on this hardware.`; it adds no such line otherwise.
